INT. DEEP-SEA HABITAT MODULE - NIGHT CYCLE

Cramped. Pipes sweat. Red emergency strip along the ceiling. Through a porthole: black water, drifting silt.

DR. IMANI KAGWE (40s, braided hair, a surgeon's steady hands) stands at a console. Behind her, COMMANDER ARVID LUNDQVIST (50s, close-cropped silver, a voice trained to be calm) holds a tablet.

LUNDQVIST
Oxygen's holding.

KAGWE
For how long?

LUNDQVIST
Nine hours.

KAGWE
That's not holding. That's a countdown.

She turns. Their eyes meet. He sets the tablet down.

LUNDQVIST
The surface team says forty-eight.

KAGWE
The surface team says what it's told to say.

LUNDQVIST
You think they're lying.

KAGWE
I think you are.

A pipe groans. A thin jet of water hisses from a seam in the wall. Neither moves.

LUNDQVIST
The seam isn't structural.

KAGWE
It's weeping brine. It's structural.

LUNDQVIST
Imani.

KAGWE
Don't.

He grabs a wrench from the wall. She steps in front of it. He tries to go around her. She blocks him. They collide, the wrench clattering to the deck.

LUNDQVIST
Let me patch it!

KAGWE
You'll shear the coupling!

He shoves her. She shoves back. He slams against the console. She slams against the bulkhead. The jet widens.

The lights flicker. Alarms begin to chirp, softly at first.

They both freeze, staring at the porthole. Beyond the glass, something large and pale slides past.

It does not slide past again.
